\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}
Analyzes a Solomon four-group design with several posttest occasions by
a mixed model for repeated measures (MMRM), and estimates the four
Solomon contrasts and the pretest effects at each occasion, and the change
in pretest sensitization from the first occasion to the last. The model is
likelihood-based, so its treatment contrasts are valid when posttests are
missing at random, for example when participants drop out depending on
their earlier scores (Fitzmaurice et al., 2011, pp. 497, 505). The pretest
effects rest instead on the assumption of the pretest center when some
pretested participants have no posttest at any occasion (see "Missing
posttests and the pretest center" below). Per-occasion analyses of
the participants still observed, and unweighted generalized estimating
equations, require the stronger assumption that the posttests are missing
completely at random (Liang & Zeger, 1986; Fitzmaurice et al., 2011, pp.
358, 498). The section "Limits" states what else the model assumes and
where its standard errors are approximate.
}
\section{Model}{

The specification follows the example of Mallinckrodt et al. (2008, p.
312), adapted to the Solomon design:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Fixed effects.} Occasion x Treatment x Pretested, all categorical.
\item \strong{Pretest adjustment.} The pretest enters as in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}:
in the pretested groups, the score minus the mean pretest of the
pretested participants in the model (each counted once; returned as
\code{pretest_mean}; with \code{pretest_center = "observed"}, the mean of every
observed pretest among the pretested participants, with or without a
posttest), and 0 in the unpretested groups, with a separate slope
at each occasion ("a full interaction of the covariate with time", p.
312). Pretests absent by design are never imputed.
\item \strong{Pretest effects.} At each occasion, the pretest effects among
controls, among treated participants, and their average compare
pretested and unpretested participants at that mean pretest, as
described in the section "The pretest effect" of \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}.
Pretest effects may fade with time (Entwisle, 1961, p. 610), and these
estimates show whether they do. Their standard errors include the
sampling variance of the mean pretest, s^2 / n for n pretested
participants, times the square of that occasion's pretest slope, and
their degrees of freedom combine those of the contrast with n - 1 by the
Welch-Satterthwaite formula (Satterthwaite, 1946; Welch, 1947). The
pretest effects were not part of the validation study below.
\item \strong{Missing posttests and the pretest center.} As in
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}} (see its section "The pretest effect when posttests
are missing"), the pretest effects depend on the mean pretest at which
they are evaluated when some pretested participants have no posttest at
any occasion; \code{pretest_center} chooses it, and \code{"observed"} needs those
participants' rows, with \code{y_post} missing. At each occasion, the
pretest effects at the two centers differ by that occasion's pretest
slope times the difference between the two means. Which center is
right depends on who was lost before the first posttest. When all four
groups lose participants alike by their baseline, the default is
unbiased, and \code{"observed"} is off by that difference. When only the
pretested groups lose them by their pretest, and the unpretested
groups' losses are unrelated to baseline, \code{"observed"} is unbiased, and
the default is off by it (the decision record posted on issue #135,
section 1). A participant with a
posttest at any occasion is analyzed, so dropout after the first
occasion leaves the two centers equal. Neither center protects the
later occasions when dropout depends on baseline beyond the earlier
posttests: the unpretested groups' model cannot see the baseline, so
their later posttests are not missing at random given what it
includes. In a check of this (\code{tools/pretest-center-check.R}, part 5:
40 data sets of 1,500 participants per group, no effects, two
occasions, a third of the second posttests lost with probability
\code{plogis(-1 - 1.5 * x)} in every group, \code{x} the baseline), the pretest main
effect was unbiased at the first occasion (0.000, Monte Carlo standard
error 0.004) and biased at the second by -0.067 (0.005) at either
center. When dropout depended on the first posttest instead, both
occasions were unbiased (-0.005 and -0.002). When a third of the
participants had no posttest at all, lost by baseline in every group,
the default was unbiased and \code{"observed"} biased by -0.18 at both
occasions; when only the pretested groups lost them by baseline, and the
unpretested at random, the reverse held (+0.18 at the default). It is a
check, not a simulation study.
\item \strong{Covariance.} Unstructured within participant, estimated by
restricted maximum likelihood (Laird & Ware, 1982). With a pretest, it
is estimated separately for pretested and unpretested participants: the
pretest adjustment makes the residual covariance conditional on the
pretest in the pretested groups only, so a single shared matrix would
be misspecified. The treated and the control group of a pretest
condition share one matrix; see "Limits".
\item \strong{Fallback.} If the unstructured model does not converge, the
heterogeneous Toeplitz, heterogeneous AR(1), and heterogeneous compound
symmetry structures are fitted, and the converged one with the smallest
AIC is used (Mallinckrodt et al., 2008, p. 312). The output names the
structure used. "Limits" says what changes after a fallback.
\item \strong{Inference.} t tests and intervals with Kenward-Roger degrees of
freedom and adjusted covariance (Kenward & Roger, 1997, as cited in
Fitzmaurice et al., 2011, p. 101), as Mallinckrodt et al. (2008, p.
312) specify, or with Satterthwaite (1946) degrees of freedom. The
Kenward-Roger adjustment depends on how the covariance is
parameterized. It is computed in its linear form (mmrm's
"Kenward-Roger-Linear"), which leaves out the term of the adjustment
that holds the second derivatives of the covariance matrix with respect
to its parameters (Sabanes Bove et al., 2026). With the unstructured
matrix and its own elements as the parameters, that term is zero, and
the computation reproduces SAS PROC MIXED. This holds for the
unstructured matrix only, not after a fallback (see "Limits").
Fitzmaurice et al. (2011, p. 101) note that the small-sample properties
of both approximations in longitudinal models "have not been
extensively studied"; the simulation study posted on issue #57 compares
them in Solomon designs.
}

The model is fitted with the mmrm package (Sabanes Bove et al., 2026),
version 0.3.13 or later, which must be installed.

A pretested participant with no pretest score in any row is excluded,
with a warning, as \code{missing_pretest = "drop"} excludes them in
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}, and the print says how many and why. For the
pretest effects, that assumes that in each pretested group those without
a pretest would have had the same mean posttests as those with one; when
their loss relates to baseline, the pretest effects are biased (see
"Pretested participants without a pretest" in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}).
Keeping them by the missing-indicator method, as
\code{missing_pretest = "indicator"} does there, waits for an amendment to
the protocol of issue #57.
}

\section{Limits}{

These limits come from the model and from the literature on it. They do
not depend on the results of a simulation study.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Dropout that depends on scores.} The standard errors rest on the
expected information, as mmrm and SAS PROC MIXED compute it, with
either choice of \code{df}. Kenward and Molenberghs (1998) show that when
dropout depends on the scores observed before it, the mean and the
covariance parameters of the normal model are no longer orthogonal (p.
242): their estimates are correlated however large the sample. Such
standard errors can then be wrong at any sample size, and Kenward and
Molenberghs recommend the observed information instead (pp. 238--239,
247). In the simulations of Maruo et al. (2020, Table 1), with 200
participants per arm, three occasions, maximum likelihood, and dropout
that depended on the previous score, the usual standard error of the
treatment difference at the last occasion was 4\% to 5\% too small with
20\% of the last scores missing, and 13\% to 17\% too small with 40\%,
when each arm had its own unstructured matrix. When the arms rightly
shared one matrix, it was within 2\% of the right value. With a pretest
in the model, a treatment contrast compares two groups that share a
matrix, and a pretest effect compares two groups whose matrices are
estimated separately. The limit therefore bears on the pretest
effects: under such dropout their standard errors can be too small.
The function does not offer standard errors from the observed
information. The printed output of a fit with a pretest and missing
posttests carries a note.
\item \strong{One covariance matrix for both arms.} The model assumes that the
treated and the control group of a pretest condition have the same
covariance matrix. With one unstructured matrix for both arms and the
Kenward-Roger method, Gosho and Maruo (2018) found that intervals for
the treatment effect covered too rarely when the arm with more dropout
had the larger variance, and that the estimate was biased as well when
the arms' correlations also differed. The covariance estimator of Mancl
and DeRouen (2001, as cited in Gosho & Maruo, 2018) did better in the
first case, and a matrix for each arm in the second. The function
offers neither.
\item \strong{After a fallback.} The fallback structures are not linear in their
parameters. For such structures, the default Kenward-Roger adjustment
of SAS PROC MIXED includes the second-derivative term, which can shrink
the standard errors and depends on the parameterization; its
\code{FIRSTORDER} option leaves the term out (SAS Institute Inc., 2009).
This function always leaves it out, so after a fallback it gives the
first-order form of the adjustment, not the default of SAS. The
structure is also chosen by AIC, which is unreliable in small samples.
With 10 participants in two groups and five occasions, AIC chose the
true structure, when that was simpler than the unstructured one, in
27\% to 73\% of complete samples and in 20\% to 66\% of samples with
dropouts (Skene & Kenward, 2010, Table II). In the simulations of
Gomez et al. (2005), Kenward-Roger tests had Type I error rates above
the nominal level whenever the structure was chosen by AIC or BIC. The
inference after a fallback has not been evaluated for this function:
in the simulation study below, the unstructured model converged in
every replication.
\item \strong{Outcomes that are not normal.} The model assumes normal residuals,
and the simulation study below drew normal outcomes. From simulations
with 30 to 42 participants in three groups, Arnau et al. (2012)
conclude that Kenward-Roger tests are compromised when the outcome is
moderately skewed, as a log-normal outcome is, most of all for the
effect of time, and that they hold up when it is normal or only
slightly skewed.
}
}

\section{Validation}{

A simulation study under a protocol posted on issue #57 before any run
(12 scenarios, 2,000 replications each; see the article "Longitudinal
Designs: Validating the Repeated-Measures Analysis") generated monotone
dropout that depended on the last posttest. With 30 to 120 participants
per group:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Coverage and Type I error.} With Kenward-Roger degrees of freedom,
95\% intervals covered from 0.937 to 0.962 of the time, and Type I
error where the true value was zero ranged from 0.041 to 0.0615.
Satterthwaite degrees of freedom performed alike.
\item \strong{Bias.} The contrasts were unbiased; the largest bias was 0.029 SD.
\item \strong{Normal reference distribution.} It gave coverage as low as 0.935
at 30 per group.
\item \strong{Shared covariance.} A covariance shared by all four groups misstated
the standard errors by up to 12\% for the unpretested contrasts and 17\%
for the pretested ones.
\item \strong{Complete-case analyses.} Per-occasion analyses of the participants
still observed were biased by up to 0.09 SD at the last occasion.
}
}

\section{Lifecycle}{

Experimental. The study's pre-specified rule for choosing the default
degrees of freedom, every tolerance met in 90\% of cells and in every cell
with 60 or more per group, was not met by either approximation (145 of
156 cells each; 97 and 96 of 104), although Monte Carlo error alone makes
the second part unlikely to be met even by a correctly calibrated method.
Kenward-Roger remains the default, as Mallinckrodt et al. (2008)
specify.
}

